Toward a Deployable Modular Starship Architecture

Thesis I: Modular Deployment Architecture of a Super-Heavy Launch Vehicle

Author: Ouadi Maakoul
Research Draft: v0.3
Status: Theoretical PhD Thesis – Chapter I (Carrier and Deployment)
Research Paradigm: Systems Engineering / Space Architecture / Optimization under Uncertainty

---

Abstract

This thesis chapter addresses the problem of transforming the payload volume of a reusable super-heavy launch vehicle—specifically the SpaceX Starship—into a modular deployment system capable of supporting on‑orbit assembly of habitable infrastructure. Rather than proposing a single architecture, the chapter frames the problem as a morphological optimization across three distinct modularity regimes: monolithic (1–3 large modules), core–periphery (a few large modules complemented by numerous small standardized units), and highly fractionated (hundreds to thousands of small modules). We introduce a formal architectural space defined by deployment door geometry, module population distribution, and packing efficiency, and we formulate the central problem as the maximization of a utility function that balances habitable volume, system mass, reliability, integration complexity, and operational risk. The chapter critically reviews historical precedents—particularly DARPA’s System F6 and NASA’s OSAM‑1/OSAM‑2—to identify failure modes that any new architecture must explicitly avoid. It further establishes a decomposition of volumetric capacity into geometric, dynamic, packable, and usable volumes, thereby separating manufacturer claims from engineering reality. The core contribution of this thesis is not a final design but a quantitative framework for determining the optimal degree of modularity N^* for a given launch vehicle. All speculative parameters (e.g., structural penalty of doors, separation Δv, deployment time) are treated as variables to be computed through simulation rather than assumed. The chapter concludes with a set of research questions, hypotheses, and a simulation plan that will guide the subsequent analysis.

---

1. Introduction and Motivation

The conventional paradigm of space infrastructure construction is launch a complete spacecraft. Under this model, the launch vehicle is a passive cargo carrier whose payload bay dimensions define the maximum size of any space system. The emergence of reusable super‑heavy launch vehicles—most notably the SpaceX Starship, with a claimed payload volume of 1{,}100\ \text{m}^3 and an internal dynamic envelope of 8\ \text{m} diameter and up to 22\ \text{m} height—introduces the possibility of a different paradigm: launch construction materials and assemble in orbit.

Previous work (Maakoul, v0.1) proposed transforming Starship’s payload volume into a modular deployment bay containing a large number of standardized “Orbital Modular Units” (OMUs). That work, however, implicitly assumed that a swarm of small modules was the desired architecture without quantitatively justifying the degree of modularity. Subsequent critical review (v0.2) incorporated historical lessons and industry trends, leading to a revised core–periphery concept in which a few large habitat modules are complemented by many small infrastructure modules. The present chapter goes one step further: we do not presume that any particular architecture is optimal. Instead, we define a broad architectural space and formulate an optimization problem whose solution will reveal the appropriate degree of modularity for a given mission.

The fundamental question addressed by Thesis I is therefore:

Given a Starship‑class reusable launch vehicle, what deployment architecture and what module population distribution (number and size of modules) maximize the utility of the resulting orbital infrastructure per unit of launch mass and per unit of assembly time?

This question is intentionally broad. It encompasses the design of the payload bay (deployment door geometry, rack configuration, ejection mechanisms), the packing problem (how many modules of a given size can be accommodated), and the interface mass penalty that scales with the number of modules. It is not concerned with the detailed internal design of individual modules—that is deferred to Thesis II—but treats modules as parameterized black boxes characterized by mass, volume, function, and interface requirements.

---

2. Historical Context and Lessons Learned

Any proposal for on‑orbit modular assembly must confront the fact that the concept has been attempted before, and has failed—not because the underlying physics is impossible, but because of integration, cost, and business‑case challenges. Two programs are particularly instructive.

2.1 DARPA System F6 (2008–2013)

System F6 (Future, Fast, Flexible, Fractionated, Free‑Flying Spacecraft) was a DARPA program that aimed to replace a monolithic spacecraft with a cluster of physically independent, standardized modules that would share power, data, and thermal resources wirelessly. DARPA awarded Phase 1 contracts to Boeing, Lockheed Martin, Northrop Grumman, and Orbital Sciences in 2008, and narrowed to Orbital Sciences for Phase 2 in 2009. The program was terminated in 2013. According to program leadership, the termination was driven primarily by the lack of an overall integrator to pull the mission together, and by the absence of a clear business case for heterogeneous, fractionated space missions (SpaceNews, 2013).

Lesson: Technical feasibility is necessary but not sufficient. The architecture must include a clear integration and management structure, and the economic value of modularity must be demonstrated relative to monolithic alternatives.

2.2 NASA OSAM‑1 and OSAM‑2/Archinaut

OSAM‑1 (On‑orbit Servicing, Assembly, and Manufacturing 1) was intended to demonstrate robotic refueling of a satellite, assembly of a communications antenna, and construction of a beam using a robotic arm. It was cancelled in 2024 after years of cost growth and schedule delays; NASA cited “continued technical, cost, and schedule challenges” and the lack of a committed partner for refueling unprepared spacecraft. OSAM‑2/Archinaut, which would have demonstrated 3D printing of two beams and deployment of a solar array on a single spacecraft, was concluded in 2023 without ever flying (NASA, 2024).

Lesson: Even much simpler assembly tasks—involving a single spacecraft and no orbital rendezvous—have proven difficult to bring to flight. A new architecture must explicitly address the coupling between technical complexity, integration burden, and cost/schedule risk.

These precedents do not invalidate the concept of modular orbital construction; they define the failure modes that a successful architecture must avoid. In particular, the proposed architecture must:

1. Provide a clear integration authority (e.g., a central command module).
2. Demonstrate a business case that does not rely on speculative servicing markets.
3. Minimize the number of distinct interfaces and keep the total interface mass low.
4. Acknowledge that the optimal number of modules may be far smaller than the maximum that can fit in the payload bay.

