3. Architectural Space Definition

We define the design space \mathcal{A} as the set of all possible deployment architectures for a given launch vehicle. An architecture A \in \mathcal{A} is fully specified by:

· A set of module classes  \mathcal{M} = \{M_1, M_2, \dots, M_k\} , each characterized by:
  · Characteristic dimension d_i (e.g., side length or diameter)
  · Mass m_i (including interface)
  · Function f_i (habitat, power, structure, etc.)
  · Interface type I_i (e.g., iSSI, HOTDOCK, IDSS)
· A population vector  \mathbf{N} = (N_1, N_2, \dots, N_k)  giving the number of modules of each class per launch.
· A deployment mechanism D, drawn from a discrete set:
  D \in \{D_{\text{slot}}, D_{\text{door}}, D_{\text{hybrid}}\}
  where D_{\text{slot}} is a narrow Pez‑dispenser slot, D_{\text{door}} is a large clamshell door (chomper), and D_{\text{hybrid}} combines both.
· A rack and retention system \mathcal{R} that determines how modules are stored and released.

The total number of modules is N = \sum_{i=1}^k N_i. The mass of the payload is

M_{\text{payload}} = \sum_{i=1}^k N_i m_i + M_{\text{rack}} + M_{\text{door}}(D),

where M_{\text{rack}} and M_{\text{door}} are mass penalties associated with the storage and deployment system.

The architecture is subject to volumetric and mass constraints imposed by the launch vehicle (Section 4).

To organize the analysis, we define three archetypal architecture classes:

· Architecture A – Monolithic: N \le 3, each module very large (4–8 m class), using full IDSS/NDS interfaces.
· Architecture B – Core–Peripheral: 2 \le N_{\text{core}} \le 10 large modules (4–8 m) and 20 \le N_{\text{peripheral}} \le 200 small modules (0.4–0.8 m), with lightweight interfaces (iSSI) on peripheral modules and heavy interfaces only on core modules.
· Architecture C – Highly Fractionated: N \ge 100, all modules small (0.4–0.8 m), using lightweight interfaces, no large pressurized volumes (or inflatable small habitats).

These three classes span the continuum from low modularity to high modularity. The optimization problem of Section 6 will determine which class—and more specifically, which population vector \mathbf{N}—is optimal.

