5. Deployment Mechanism Analysis

The choice of deployment mechanism D is a central design variable because it influences structural mass, reliability, and the types of modules that can be deployed.

5.1 Option D_{\text{slot}} – Narrow Pez Slot

SpaceX has demonstrated sequential deployment of Starlink satellites through a narrow rectangular slot near the nose of Starship. This mechanism has the advantage of minimal structural penalty—no large door that must withstand reentry—and has been flight‑tested (Flights 10–13). However, the slot size limits the maximum module cross‑section. For a slot of width w_s and height h_s, only modules with

\max(d_i) \le \min(w_s, h_s)

can be deployed. In practice, this limits D_{\text{slot}} to small peripheral modules (0.4–0.8 m).

5.2 Option D_{\text{door}} – Large Clamshell Door (Chomper)

A large clamshell door, similar to the Space Shuttle payload bay doors, provides access to the full internal diameter and can deploy modules up to 8 m in size. However, it imposes a significant structural mass penalty because the door must be reinforced to handle launch loads and reentry thermal stresses, and the opening weakens the cylindrical pressure vessel (if the payload bay is pressurized) or the structural integrity of the vehicle. Preliminary estimates from the literature suggest a dry mass increase on the order of 10–15% of the vehicle’s dry mass. This mass penalty is a key parameter to be determined by finite element analysis.

5.3 Option D_{\text{hybrid}} – Combined Slot and Door

A hybrid architecture includes one or more narrow slots for small modules and a larger door for core modules. This allows a mix of module sizes but increases complexity: two different deployment mechanisms must be accommodated, and the structural reinforcements for the door may interfere with the slot placement.

For the present thesis, we treat the deployment mechanism as a discrete variable and do not presuppose which is optimal. The mass penalty of each option will be estimated through structural modeling, and the resulting M_{\text{door}}(D) will be inserted into the mass constraint.

---

6. Interface Mass Penalty and the Marginal Cost of Modularity

A critical insight from the historical review is that each additional module brings not only its own mass but also the mass of its interface(s). The interface is a mechatronic system that provides mechanical connection, power transfer, data transfer, and possibly thermal and fluid transfer. Lightweight interfaces such as iSSI weigh about 0.9 kg, while full IDSS/NDS mechanisms for crew passage weigh hundreds of kilograms. Even ignoring the heavy IDSS, if a module requires two interfaces (one on each side), the mass penalty per module is on the order of 1.8–3.0 kg. For a module of mass 50 kg, this is 4–6% overhead—not negligible.

More importantly, the total interface mass scales linearly with the number of modules:

M_{\text{interfaces}} = \sum_{i=1}^k N_i \cdot \overline{m}_{I,i},

where \overline{m}_{I,i} is the average interface mass per module of class i. This term must be subtracted from the payload mass budget.

The marginal cost of modularity can be expressed as:

\frac{\partial U}{\partial N} = \text{(benefit of one additional module)} - \text{(mass, power, complexity, risk of one additional interface)}.

This marginal cost is positive for small N (more modules increase redundancy and flexibility) but may become negative for large N because the interface overhead and integration complexity dominate. The existence of an interior optimum N^* is a hypothesis to be tested by the optimization.

---

7. Optimization Framework: Utility Maximization

The core of Thesis I is the formulation of an optimization problem that answers the question: What architecture A^* maximizes the utility of the orbital infrastructure per unit launch mass?

7.1 Utility Function

We define a scalar utility function U(A) that captures the value of the deployed system. Many possible utility functions exist; here we propose a multi‑criteria utility that combines the most important outcomes:

U(A) = \frac{V_{\text{habitable}}(A) \cdot T_{\text{mission}} \cdot F_{\text{reliability}}(A)}
{M_{\text{total}}(A) \cdot D_{\text{effective}}(A) \cdot C_{\text{integration}}(A)},

where:

· V_{\text{habitable}} is the total pressurized habitable volume after assembly (m³).
· T_{\text{mission}} is the planned operational lifetime (years).
· F_{\text{reliability}} is the probability that the system remains functional throughout the mission (dimensionless).
· M_{\text{total}} is the total mass delivered to orbit, including all modules, racks, interfaces, and deployment mechanisms (kg).
· D_{\text{effective}} is the effective radiation dose rate inside the habitat after shielding (mSv/day).
· C_{\text{integration}} is a dimensionless integration complexity factor, modeled as a function of the number of modules and the heterogeneity of interfaces.

This utility function is intentionally simple; in practice it may be replaced by a weighted sum of multiple objectives, but it captures the fundamental trade‑offs.

7.2 Constraints

The optimization is subject to the constraints derived in Section 4 and 5:

\sum_{i=1}^k N_i V_i \le \eta_{\text{rack}}\, \eta_{\text{packing}}\, V_{\text{dynamic}},

\sum_{i=1}^k N_i m_i + M_{\text{rack}} + M_{\text{door}}(D) \le M_{\text{payload, max}},

N_i \ge 0, \quad N_i \in \mathbb{Z}^+,

D \in \{D_{\text{slot}}, D_{\text{door}}, D_{\text{hybrid}}\}.

Additional operational constraints may be imposed, such as:

· Minimum continuous power P_{\text{min}} for crew support.
· Maximum assembly time T_{\text{assembly}} \le T_{\text{max}}.
· Maximum collision probability P_{\text{collision}} \le \epsilon.
· Radiation dose limit D_{\text{effective}} \le D_{\text{limit}}.

7.3 Optimal Modularity N^*

The optimization yields an optimal architecture:

A^* = \arg\max_{A \in \mathcal{A}} U(A) \quad \text{subject to constraints},

and the corresponding optimal population  \mathbf{N}^* . The total number of modules N^* = \sum_i N_i^* is the optimal degree of modularity for the given launch vehicle and mission.

We hypothesize that N^* will fall in one of three regimes:

· If N^* \le 3, the monolithic architecture is optimal.
· If 3 < N^* < 100, the core–periphery architecture is optimal.
· If N^* \ge 100, a highly fractionated architecture is optimal.

The exact value depends on the detailed mass and volume models, the interface mass, the radiation shielding requirement, and the integration complexity model. The primary scientific contribution of this thesis is the determination of N^* for Starship under realistic assumptions.

