Appendix A: Volume Decomposition Formalism and Proofs

A.1 Hierarchical Volume Definitions

We define a nested hierarchy of volumes characterizing the payload accommodation of a reusable launch vehicle. Let \Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^3 denote the spatial domain of the payload bay.

Definition A.1 (Geometric Volume). The geometric volume V_{\text{geometric}} is the Lebesgue measure of the interior domain bounded by the physical walls of the payload bay:

V_{\text{geometric}} = \int_{\Omega} dV = \mu(\Omega)

where \mu denotes the three-dimensional Lebesgue measure. For a right circular cylinder of inner diameter D_{\text{inner}} and total height H_{\text{total}}:

V_{\text{geometric}} = \frac{\pi D_{\text{inner}}^2}{4} H_{\text{total}}

Definition A.2 (Dynamic Envelope Volume). The dynamic envelope volume V_{\text{dynamic}} is the subset of \Omega accessible to payloads after accounting for:

· Structural wall thickness and stiffening members
· Thermal protection system (TPS) intrusions
· Plumbing, wiring, and actuation runs
· Dynamic deflection envelopes under launch loads
· Manufacturing tolerances

Formally, V_{\text{dynamic}} \subset V_{\text{geometric}}, with:

V_{\text{dynamic}} = \eta_{\text{envelope}} V_{\text{geometric}}

where \eta_{\text{envelope}} \in (0,1) is the envelope efficiency factor.

Definition A.3 (Packable Volume). The packable volume V_{\text{packable}} is the volume remaining after installation of the modular rack system:

V_{\text{packable}} = \eta_{\text{rack}} V_{\text{dynamic}}

where \eta_{\text{rack}} accounts for rack structural volume, deployment mechanism volume, thermal control loops, and access corridors.

Definition A.4 (Usable Module Volume). The usable module volume V_{\text{usable}} is the volume actually occupied by spacecraft modules:

V_{\text{usable}} = \eta_{\text{packing}} V_{\text{packable}}

where \eta_{\text{packing}} is the packing efficiency, defined as the ratio of module volume to containing volume.

A.2 Theorem: Packing Efficiency Bounds

Theorem A.1 (Upper Bound on Packing Efficiency). For a set of non-overlapping convex modules of total volume V_{\text{modules}} packed within a cylindrical container of volume V_{\text{container}}, the packing efficiency satisfies:

\eta_{\text{packing}} \equiv \frac{V_{\text{modules}}}{V_{\text{container}}} \leq \eta_{\text{dense}}

where \eta_{\text{dense}} is the maximum packing density of the corresponding regular arrangement.

Proof. The theorem follows from the definition of packing density in discrete geometry (Hales, 1998; Conway & Sloane, 1999). For congruent cubes in a cylinder, the maximum packing density is bounded above by the face-centered cubic packing density \eta_{\text{FCC}} = \frac{\pi}{3\sqrt{2}} \approx 0.7405 for spheres. For cubes, numerical studies indicate \eta_{\text{max}} \leq 0.78 for container dimensions much larger than cube dimensions (Li et al., 2015). The boundary effects of the cylindrical container further reduce achievable density. \square

Corollary A.1 (Practical Packing Efficiency). For cubical modules of side length s in a cylinder of diameter D, with D \gg s, the practical packing efficiency satisfies:

\eta_{\text{packing}} \lesssim 0.70

accounting for boundary curvature, rack structural members, and clearance tolerances.

A.3 Volume Decomposition Chain

Combining the hierarchical definitions:

\boxed{
V_{\text{usable}} = \eta_{\text{packing}} \cdot \eta_{\text{rack}} \cdot \eta_{\text{envelope}} \cdot V_{\text{geometric}}
}

For the nominal Starship geometry (D_{\text{inner}} = 8 m, H = 22 m):

V_{\text{geometric}} = \frac{\pi (8)^2}{4} (22) = 352\pi \approx 1{,}106 \text{ m}^3

Assuming \eta_{\text{envelope}} = 0.95, \eta_{\text{rack}} = 0.80, \eta_{\text{packing}} = 0.65:

V_{\text{usable}} = 0.65 \times 0.80 \times 0.95 \times 1{,}106 \approx 546 \text{ m}^3

This represents approximately 49% of the geometric volume—a significant reduction that must be accounted for in any realistic capacity assessment.

