Appendix B: Optimization Problem Formalization

B.1 General Formulation

Let \mathcal{A} denote the architectural space. An architecture A \in \mathcal{A} is defined by the tuple:

A = (\mathcal{M}, \mathbf{N}, D, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{I})

where:

· \mathcal{M} = \{M_1, M_2, \ldots, M_k\} is the set of module classes
· \mathbf{N} = (N_1, N_2, \ldots, N_k) is the population vector
· D \in \{D_{\text{slot}}, D_{\text{door}}, D_{\text{hybrid}}\} is the deployment mechanism
· \mathcal{R} is the rack configuration
· \mathcal{I} is the interface suite

B.2 Utility Function Definition

Definition B.1 (Utility Function). The utility U: \mathcal{A} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^+ is defined as:

U(A) = \frac{V_{\text{habitable}}(A) \cdot T_{\text{mission}}(A) \cdot F_{\text{reliability}}(A)}
{M_{\text{total}}(A) \cdot D_{\text{effective}}(A) \cdot C_{\text{integration}}(A)}

where each term is defined as follows:

B.2.1 Habitable Volume:

V_{\text{habitable}}(A) = \sum_{i \in \mathcal{H}} N_i V_{\text{habitable},i}

where \mathcal{H} \subseteq \{1,\ldots,k\} denotes the subset of module classes providing pressurized habitable volume, and V_{\text{habitable},i} is the net habitable volume of a single module of class i, calculated per NASA-STD-3001 excluding stowage, equipment, and structural intrusions.

B.2.2 Mission Lifetime:

T_{\text{mission}}(A) = \min_i T_{\text{design},i}

where T_{\text{design},i} is the design lifetime of module class i. The system lifetime is limited by the shortest-lived critical component.

B.2.3 Reliability:

For a system of N modules with failure rates \lambda_i (failures per year):

F_{\text{reliability}}(A) = \exp\left(-\sum_{i=1}^k N_i \lambda_i T_{\text{mission}}\right)

if all modules are required for operation. For redundant architectures with m-of-n redundancy:

F_{\text{reliability}} = \sum_{j=m}^{n} \binom{n}{j} R^j (1-R)^{n-j}

where R = e^{-\lambda T} is the single-module reliability.

B.2.4 Total Mass:

M_{\text{total}}(A) = \sum_{i=1}^k N_i (m_i + m_{I,i}) + M_{\text{rack}}(\mathcal{R}) + M_{\text{door}}(D) + M_{\text{margin}}

where:

· m_i is the dry mass of module class i
· m_{I,i} is the interface mass per module (including both active and passive sides)
· M_{\text{rack}}(\mathcal{R}) is the rack structural mass
· M_{\text{door}}(D) is the deployment door mass penalty
· M_{\text{margin}} is a mass margin (typically 5–15%)

B.2.5 Effective Radiation Dose:

D_{\text{effective}}(A) = \frac{\sum_{i \in \mathcal{H}} N_i V_i D_i}{\sum_{i \in \mathcal{H}} N_i V_i}

where D_i is the dose rate inside module class i, calculated as:

D_i = D_0 \exp\left(-\frac{t_{\text{shield},i}}{\lambda_{\text{attenuation}}}\right)

with D_0 the unshielded GCR dose rate, t_{\text{shield},i} the areal shielding thickness (g/cm²), and \lambda_{\text{attenuation}} the attenuation length for the shielding material.

B.2.6 Integration Complexity:

C_{\text{integration}}(A) = \left(1 + \frac{N}{N_{\text{ref}}}\right)^\alpha \cdot \left(1 + \frac{|\mathcal{I}|}{|\mathcal{I}_{\text{ref}}|}\right)^\beta

where:

· N = \sum_i N_i is the total number of modules
· N_{\text{ref}} is a reference module count (e.g., 10)
· |\mathcal{I}| is the number of distinct interface types
· \alpha \in [1.5, 2.5] captures the growth of integration complexity with module count (reflecting the combinatorial growth of pairwise interactions)
· \beta \in [0.5, 1.5] captures the complexity penalty for heterogeneous interfaces

B.3 Constraint Formalization

Constraint C1 (Volume):

\sum_{i=1}^k N_i V_{\text{bounding},i} \leq \eta_{\text{packing}} \eta_{\text{rack}} \eta_{\text{envelope}} V_{\text{geometric}}

where V_{\text{bounding},i} is the bounding box volume of module class i (including clearances).

Constraint C2 (Mass):

M_{\text{total}}(A) \leq M_{\text{payload,max}}(B)

where M_{\text{payload,max}}(B) is the maximum payload mass for Starship Block B (e.g., 35 t for Block 2, 100 t for Block 3, 200 t for Block 4 projected).

Constraint C3 (Power):

P_{\text{total}}(A) \equiv \sum_{i=1}^k N_i P_{\text{generate},i} \geq P_{\text{required}}

where P_{\text{required}} is the minimum continuous power for crew operations (e.g., 30 kW for 4 crew per DSH baseline).

Constraint C4 (Radiation):

D_{\text{effective}}(A) \leq D_{\text{limit}}

where D_{\text{limit}} is the maximum allowable dose rate (e.g., 1.3 mSv/day for missions >60 days).

Constraint C5 (Assembly Time):

T_{\text{assembly}}(A) \leq T_{\text{max}}

where T_{\text{assembly}} is modeled as:

T_{\text{assembly}} = \frac{N_{\text{docking}}}{N_{\text{arms}} \cdot f_{\text{docking}}} + T_{\text{overhead}}

with N_{\text{docking}} the number of docking operations, N_{\text{arms}} the number of robotic arms, f_{\text{docking}} the docking frequency (dockings/hour per arm), and T_{\text{overhead}} includes deployment time, phasing time, and contingency margins.

Constraint C6 (Collision Probability):

P_{\text{collision}}(A) \leq \epsilon

where P_{\text{collision}} is estimated from the deployment and rendezvous dynamics (see Appendix F).

Constraint C7 (Integer Constraints):

N_i \in \mathbb{Z}^+, \quad i = 1, \ldots, k

B.4 Optimization Problem Statement

The complete optimization problem is:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
& \underset{A \in \mathcal{A}}{\text{maximize}}
& & U(A) \\
& \text{subject to}
& & \text{C1--C7}
\end{aligned}
}

This is a mixed-integer nonlinear programming (MINLP) problem due to:

· Integer decision variables N_i
· Discrete deployment mechanism choice D
· Nonlinear utility function and constraints
· Non-convex feasible region (due to packing constraints and reliability terms)

