Appendix C: Proof of Optimal Modularity Existence

C.1 Marginal Utility of Additional Modules

Definition C.1 (Marginal Utility). The marginal utility of adding one module of class j to an architecture A is:

\Delta U_j(A) = U(A + e_j) - U(A)

where A + e_j denotes the architecture with N_j \rightarrow N_j + 1.

Theorem C.1 (Diminishing Marginal Utility). Under the assumptions of the utility function in Appendix B, for sufficiently large N, the marginal utility \Delta U_j(A) becomes negative for all module classes j.

Proof. Consider the dominant terms in U(A) as N \rightarrow \infty:

U(A) \sim \frac{C_1}{N \cdot N^\alpha} = \frac{C_1}{N^{1+\alpha}}

where C_1 is a constant capturing the numerator (habitable volume saturates as N exceeds the volume constraint), N in the denominator comes from M_{\text{total}} \propto N, and N^\alpha comes from C_{\text{integration}} \propto N^\alpha with \alpha > 0.

The marginal utility for large N:

\Delta U_j \sim \frac{\partial}{\partial N} \left( \frac{C_1}{N^{1+\alpha}} \right) = -C_1 (1+\alpha) N^{-(2+\alpha)} < 0

Thus, \Delta U_j < 0 for sufficiently large N. \square

Theorem C.2 (Existence of Interior Optimum). If:

1. There exists at least one module class j such that \Delta U_j(A) > 0 for N_j = 0 (i.e., adding the first module of that class increases utility), and
2. \Delta U_j(A) < 0 for sufficiently large N_j (from Theorem C.1),

then there exists an optimal finite population vector \mathbf{N}^* that maximizes U(A).

Proof. The feasible set is finite (bounded by the mass and volume constraints, with integer populations). The utility function is well-defined for all feasible architectures. By the extreme value theorem applied to finite sets, a maximum exists. The conditions ensure the maximum is not at the boundary N_j = 0 or N_j \rightarrow \infty. \square

Corollary C.1 (Optimal Modularity N^*). The optimal total module count is:

N^* = \sum_{i=1}^k N_i^*

where \mathbf{N}^* = (N_1^*, \ldots, N_k^*) is the solution to the optimization problem.

