Appendix D: Interface Mass Penalty Formalization

D.1 Interface Requirements per Module

Definition D.1 (Interface Requirement Matrix). For a module of class i, the interface requirements are specified by a vector:

\mathbf{I}_i = (I_{M}, I_{P}, I_{D}, I_{T}, I_{F})_i

where:

· I_{M} = mechanical interface (docking, structural connection)
· I_{P} = power interface (voltage, current capacity)
· I_{D} = data interface (bandwidth, protocol)
· I_{T} = thermal interface (heat rejection capacity)
· I_{F} = fluid interface (propellant, atmosphere, water)

Definition D.2 (Interface Mass Function). The mass of the interface for module class i is:

m_{I,i} = \sum_{j \in \{M,P,D,T,F\}} m_j(I_{j,i})

where m_j(I_{j,i}) is the mass contribution of interface type j at the performance level specified by I_{j,i}.

D.2 Empirical Interface Mass Data

Based on published specifications:

Interface Mass (active + passive) Power Data Thermal Mechanical
iSSI (iBOSS) 0.9 kg 5 kW @ 100 V 1 Gbit/s 5 W/K 6,000 N axial
HOTDOCK 1.5 kg ~500 W 10 Mbit/s ~2 W/K High tolerance
SIROM 1.5 kg 1 kW 100 Mbit/s Active Robotic capture
IDSS/NDS 300–500 kg Multi-kW High Active 0.8 m passage

D.3 Total Interface Mass

Theorem D.1 (Linear Interface Mass Growth). For an architecture with population vector \mathbf{N}, the total interface mass is:

M_{\text{interfaces}} = \sum_{i=1}^k N_i \cdot \overline{m}_{I,i}

where \overline{m}_{I,i} is the average interface mass per module of class i, accounting for the fact that some interfaces are shared between adjacent modules.

Proof. Each module requires at least one interface connection. If modules are connected in a linear chain, each module (except endpoints) requires two interfaces, but the mass is accounted as one full interface per module (the "half" interfaces at each end are negligible for large N). For a fully connected network (each module connects to all others), the mass would scale as N(N-1)/2, but this is never realized in practice. The linear growth model is the appropriate approximation for actual assembly topologies. \square

Corollary D.1 (Interface Mass Fraction). The interface mass fraction of the total payload is:

f_{\text{interface}} = \frac{M_{\text{interfaces}}}{M_{\text{payload,total}}}

For small modules (50 kg) with iSSI interfaces (0.9 kg), f_{\text{interface}} \approx 1.8\%. For the same modules with IDSS interfaces (300 kg), f_{\text{interface}} \approx 86\% — prohibitive. This demonstrates why lightweight interfaces are essential for small-module architectures.

