Appendix F: Deployment Dynamics and Collision Risk

F.1 Separation Dynamics

Definition F.1 (Separation Impulse). Upon release, a module receives a separation velocity \Delta v_{\text{sep}} from the deployment mechanism (springs, pushers). The separation distance after time t is:

d_{\text{sep}}(t) = \Delta v_{\text{sep}} \cdot t + \frac{1}{2} a_{\text{relative}} t^2

where a_{\text{relative}} is the differential acceleration between the module and the carrier due to:

· Gravitational gradient (a_{\text{gravity-gradient}} \approx \frac{3GM}{R^3} d)
· Atmospheric drag (if LEO)
· Solar radiation pressure

Definition F.2 (Safe Separation Criterion). Two objects are safely separated if:

d_{\text{sep}} \geq d_{\text{min}} + \sigma_{\text{position}}

where d_{\text{min}} is the minimum acceptable distance (e.g., 100 m for proximity operations) and \sigma_{\text{position}} is the position uncertainty.

F.2 Collision Probability Model

For a cloud of N modules deployed sequentially, the collision probability during deployment can be approximated by:

P_{\text{collision}} \approx \frac{1}{2} N (N-1) \cdot \frac{\sigma_{\text{cross-section}}}{A_{\text{shell}}}

where:

· \sigma_{\text{cross-section}} is the collision cross-section between two modules (\approx 2A_{\text{module}} for random orientation)
· A_{\text{shell}} is the area of the spherical shell within which the modules are distributed

For N = 200 modules of cross-section 0.5 m² each, distributed in a shell of radius 10 km:

A_{\text{shell}} = 4\pi (10,000)^2 \approx 1.26 \times 10^9 \text{ m}^2

P_{\text{collision}} \approx \frac{1}{2} \times 200 \times 199 \times \frac{1.0}{1.26 \times 10^9} \approx 1.6 \times 10^{-5}

This is above typical safety thresholds (10⁻⁶), indicating that active collision avoidance and/or larger separation distances are required.

F.3 Deployment Time Model

The total deployment time for N modules with ejection interval \Delta t is:

T_{\text{deployment}} = N \cdot \Delta t + T_{\text{overhead}}

For N = 200 and \Delta t = 60 s:

T_{\text{deployment}} = 200 \times 60 = 12,000 \text{ s} = 3.33 \text{ hours}

During this time, the carrier undergoes orbital precession and J2 perturbation, causing the deployment cloud to spread. The characteristic spread due to J2 over one orbit (T = 90 min) is:

\Delta x \approx \frac{3}{2} J_2 \left(\frac{R_E}{a}\right)^2 v T \approx 0.05 \text{ km}

which is small compared to the separation distances, so the modules remain approximately co-orbital.

