Appendix G: Radiation Shielding Scaling Laws

G.1 Surface-to-Volume Ratio

Theorem G.1 (Surface-to-Volume Scaling). For a family of geometrically similar modules of characteristic dimension L, the surface-to-volume ratio scales as:

\frac{A}{V} \propto \frac{1}{L}

Proof. For a solid of characteristic dimension L:

A \propto L^2, \quad V \propto L^3

\frac{A}{V} \propto \frac{L^2}{L^3} = \frac{1}{L}

\square

Corollary G.1 (Shielding Mass Scaling). The shielding mass per unit habitable volume is:

\frac{M_{\text{shield}}}{V_{\text{habitable}}} = \rho_{\text{shield}} \cdot t_{\text{shield}} \cdot \frac{A}{V} \propto \frac{t_{\text{shield}}}{L}

Thus, for a fixed shielding thickness t_{\text{shield}} (required for a given dose reduction), smaller modules require disproportionately more shielding mass per unit volume.

G.2 Numerical Example

For modules of different sizes with a shielding requirement of 20 g/cm² CFRP (50% GCR dose reduction):

Module Size Radius (m) Surface/Volume (m⁻¹) Shielding Mass per m³ Habitable (kg)
0.4 m cube 0.2 (equiv.) 15.0 30,000
0.8 m cube 0.4 (equiv.) 7.5 15,000
4 m sphere 4.0 0.75 1,500
8 m sphere 8.0 0.375 750

This demonstrates the fundamental geometric advantage of large habitat modules: an 8 m module requires 40 times less shielding mass per unit volume than a 0.4 m module.

