Appendix J: Comparison with Historical Programs

J.1 System F6 Architecture

DARPA's System F6 proposed a fully fractionated architecture with:

· Number of modules: 5–10 per cluster
· Module sizes: ~100–500 kg each
· Interface: proprietary wireless (no physical docking initially)
· Integration: Central integrator absent (cited as failure cause)

Analysis: F6's modularity was in the low-to-moderate range (5–10 modules), but the lack of physical docking meant the cluster could not form a rigid structure. The proposed core–periphery architecture in this thesis explicitly includes physical docking via iSSI/HOTDOCK interfaces and a central core module serving as integrator.

J.2 OSAM-1 Architecture

NASA's OSAM-1 proposed:

· Single spacecraft with robotic arm
· One refueling operation
· One antenna assembly
· One beam construction

Analysis: OSAM-1's scope was far simpler than the proposed architecture, yet it was cancelled due to cost and schedule growth. This underscores the importance of business case and integration management. The proposed architecture must demonstrate that the modular approach provides value beyond what monolithic stations (Starlab, Haven) already offer.

J.3 Starlab / Vast Haven-2 Baselines

Current commercial stations represent the "monolithic" extreme:

· Starlab: 2 modules, 8 m diameter each, 450 m³ total pressurized volume
· Haven-2: Sequentially larger monoliths, ~100 m³ (Haven-1) to ~350 m³ (Haven-2 first module)

The proposed core–periphery architecture seeks to match or exceed these habitable volumes while enabling incremental growth and redundancy through the peripheral module population.

