Appendix M: Glossary of Terms

Core Module: A large (4–8 m class) module providing primary habitable volume, laboratory space, or central command functions. Uses heavy interfaces (IDSS/NDS).

Peripheral Module: A small (0.4–0.8 m class) module providing infrastructure functions (power, thermal, structural, robotic). Uses lightweight interfaces (iSSI/HOTDOCK).

Core–Periphery Architecture: An architecture combining a small number of core modules with a larger number of peripheral modules.

Fractionated Spacecraft: A spacecraft whose functions are distributed across multiple physically independent modules.

Modular Deployment Bay (MDB): The transformed payload volume of Starship, containing racks, deployment mechanisms, and module interfaces.

Modular Orbital Deployment Vehicle (MODV): The Starship-derived carrier optimized for modular deployment.

Orbital Modular Unit (OMU): A standardized spacecraft module forming the basic building block of the architecture.

Packing Efficiency (\eta_{\text{packing}}): The ratio of module volume to containing volume.

Interface Mass Penalty: The mass overhead associated with docking and connection interfaces, scaling with module count.

Integration Complexity: The management and coordination burden associated with operating many independent modules.

Optimal Modularity (N^*): The total number of modules that maximizes the utility function for a given launch vehicle and mission.

